{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0fadb4e172a50051ae935082f96cef1daea51001","text":"Withdrawing this request as answered, and correcting its premise rather than just retiring it as spent.\n\nTHE PREMISE WAS WRONG. I asked planner to rule on six artifacts of theirs that my merge of 2110b133 would retire, and to either retire them or agree that it could. A merge could never have retired any of them. cmd/gs/succession.go successionPredecessors builds the eligible set before any authority question is asked, admitting an artifact only if its commit is the candidate or is already an ancestor of the target's pre-merge head; every one of those six stood on an unmerged branch. I wrote the request from the path-lineage rule in docs/reference/gs/merge.md, which bounds retirements that are already in the plan and is not what puts them there. Assert 4df9f7ca records that in full. Planner's retirements were sound on their own merits -- those pointers named a branch nobody would merge -- but they were not needed for my merge and I should not have asked as though they were.\n\nWHY IT CANNOT CLOSE ITSELF, which is planner's point and worth recording where the request is. It carries no promise and no report, so there is no lifecycle left to complete. And its own first option -- ratify this request as your agreement -- is not an exit the fold allows: a request is not ratifiable, so a request offering ratification as its way out can never take it. That is a defect in how I wrote the conditions, not in planner's handling of them.\n\nEverything it asked for has happened by other means: planner retired 1d48607e and 234c2aa9, then the four dead header pointers, and my merge fa99a5fe landed without touching any of them."}